WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.340 position:58%
Yanela Frias doesn't hesitate when
asked what she loves about working at

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.370 position:56%
Prudential.
From a cultural perspective, we're a

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:14.090 position:63%
purpose driven organization, and that is
really meaningful to me.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:19.130 position:61%
It is a group of really smart people
working together, collaborating to meet

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.680 position:61%
the challenges and solve the challenges
of our customers.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:24.710 position:58%
And that makes work interesting every
day.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:31.190 position:63%
The interesting days have compounded for
27 years into an extraordinary career.

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.820
I started in finance.
I spent about 19 years through the

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.370
finance organization.
I like to say I grew up in finance.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.630 position:61%
I spent a number of years in the
corporate Treasury organization where I

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:45.980 position:63%
probably grew the most there, and then I
went off and was CFO for a couple of

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.280 position:59%
businesses, both the life business and
the annuities business.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.290
And then I said, I want to run a
business.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:56.120 position:59%
So that is quite non-traditional.
But I really wanted to experience what

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:59.630
it was like to run a P&amp;L.
But did you always have clarity?

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:00.800
Did you always know,
sort of,

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.860 position:70%
I'm here, but I know where I'm going to next?
Directionally.

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:06.100
To me, it wasn't
I want to be CFO,

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:10.010 position:59%
I want to be CEO.
To me, it's always been I want to have

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:14.240
growing responsibilities where
I'm challenged every day, where I'm

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:20.150 position:69%
adding value and where I'm making an impact.
In March 2024, Yanela Frias took on her

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:24.800 position:58%
most challenging assignment so far as
Prudential's chief financial officer.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.490 position:59%
CEO Charlie Lowrey says putting her in
that position was an easy call. She

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:35.030 position:61%
was the right person at the right time.
She understands the business side.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:39.350 position:58%
She understands the functional side.
She understands the financial side of

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:42.410
things.
And given that combination of

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:47.930 position:59%
experiences, we thought she would be a
fantastic CFO and she's turning out to

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.210
just knock the cover off the ball.
She's been great.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:55.040 position:58%
It's a big job and Frias has embraced
it.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:00.530 position:63%
I think one of the biggest surprises and
shouldn't have been a surprise, but

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:06.110 position:63%
biggest awareness for me was how
vast the role is and how everything that

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:10.130 position:61%
happens in the organization in one way,
shape or form crosses my desk.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.910 position:63%
Prudential has become a diversified
global financial services company with a

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:18.450 position:59%
complex mix of businesses contributing
to its success.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:23.880 position:56%
Its future hinges on leaders who can
connect the dots, who can mold a

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.560 position:58%
dynamic array of people, products and
services into a purposeful and

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.700
profitable big picture.
Yanela Frias is an accomplished dot

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.890 position:63%
connector.
Need any help on the capital piece or is

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.740 position:70%
that...well the capital piece is interactive.
Having run the retirement business,

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:44.460 position:58%
having run the group business, having
been many years in our Treasury

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.700 position:61%
organization.
Understanding the opportunities that we

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.290 position:58%
have as an organization and as a
business, but also how do we meet the

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:56.250 position:58%
needs of customers and do so in a way
that creates value for everyone?

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.310 position:61%
And that's where connecting the dots in
terms of here are the risks we're

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.830 position:63%
taking.
How are we getting paid for those risks?

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.860 position:61%
How are we making sure that we meet our
promises?

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.730 position:61%
We're in the business of making long
term promises, so focusing on financial

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:13.170 position:61%
strength and being here for another 150
years is top of mind for us.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:28.540
Prudential's reputation rests on
consistency.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.450 position:58%
Like the Rock of Gibraltar built into
its logo.

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:36.070 position:67%
But when Charlie Lowrey became CEO in 2018,
he realized that he had to set the

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:41.260
company on a bold new course.
This company needed to transform,

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:46.870 position:61%
and there was a lot of hard work that
needed to be done in order to transform

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.940 position:56%
it.
We've been on a journey to be higher

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:51.040 position:56%
growth, more nimble and more capital
efficient.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:55.330 position:63%
There's been a lot of pressure on the
banks since the financial crisis, and so

00:03:55.330 --> 00:04:00.340 position:61%
what you've had is the life insurance
companies filling a lot of that savings

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:02.380 position:63%
gap.
And in addition, you have the retirement

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:04.570 position:63%
crisis.
So you have the life insurance companies

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:08.740
also rising to address that
savings for retirement gap.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:12.370 position:59%
And then on the asset management side,
you've seen a lot more private credit

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:17.320 position:58%
because they are working to fill that
gap that has been left in lending.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:21.820 position:59%
And so now what you're seeing is that
the two industries are coming together

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:27.850 position:64%
and it works very well because they both
need AUM. We have the ability to generate

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:30.610 position:63%
liabilities.
We have an ability to invest that in one

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:33.190 position:56%
of the world class asset managers we
have.

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:37.810 position:61%
We have an amazing retirement business.
Our goal is to continue to grow.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.490 position:61%
We have a lot of opportunities in the
marketplace and we need to do that in a

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:46.840 position:64%
thoughtful way.
On Lowrey's watch, Prudential streamlined

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:52.300 position:59%
its processes and reshaped its product
line, putting it on track for record

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:57.320 position:64%
revenue in 2024.
Lowrey will step down as the CEO in April

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:02.080 position:58%
2025. While he'll stay for another 18
months as executive chair,

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:06.790 position:61%
it will fall to the new CEO, Andy
Sullivan and the CFO Frias, to keep the

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:10.390 position:59%
positive momentum going.
We have lowered our market sensitivity

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:15.520 position:61%
by reinsuring a lot of different blocks
of business in annuities and life, and

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.240 position:59%
we become more nimble through a lot of
the transformative work we did through

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.880 position:63%
expense savings, through reorganization,
etc.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:26.950 position:63%
That work has all been done, but we take
that and we really accelerate into a

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:30.700 position:58%
growth agenda, which is what Andy and
Yanela and others are going to do.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:35.710 position:59%
Prudential is poised to benefit from a
major demographic shift with a record

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:40.900 position:61%
number of Americans reaching age 65.
More people than ever before are in the

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:44.530 position:61%
market for retirement, planning, advice
and solutions.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:47.500 position:56%
As the company's retirement business
thrives,

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:52.000 position:58%
Its investment arm, PGIM, has boosted
assets under management by scaling up

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.440 position:61%
its holdings in alternatives.
But even with a mandate to hit the gas,

00:05:56.740 --> 00:06:01.240 position:59%
a CFO must know when to use the brake.
We're an insurance company.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:04.930 position:58%
We're in the business of taking risk.
We are looking to diversify our risk

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:08.140 position:59%
profile because it's important to have
that diversification.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.610 position:58%
But in order to grow, we have to take
risks.

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:13.510 position:61%
They have to be prudent.
They have to be thoughtful, and we have

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:15.370
to manage them appropriately.
Are you a CFO

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:20.560 position:63%
that sort of is in haste to move? Or
again, is it a little bit more cautious,

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:22.750 position:56%
and I like to have a lot of homework
done before I pull the trigger?

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:28.550 position:58%
I'm in the middle.
I know that's that's the easy answer,

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:31.490 position:61%
but no.
So I am incredibly results oriented and

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:35.150 position:59%
focused on outcomes.
And I like to move quickly, but I like

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:40.040 position:61%
to move when I have enough information.
But I'm a big believer in, you know, if

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:44.060 position:59%
you have 100% perfect information, you
studied the problem too long.

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:48.680 position:59%
Along with risk management,
expense management will be critical to

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:51.890
Prudential's continued success.
I don't get the sense you have to

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:56.210 position:67%
instill that in Yanela.
I think she struck me as a -- Oh no, you do

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:59.250 position:61%
not have to instill that in Yanela that
that's part of it.

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:05.360 position:63%
That's part of the DNA, part of her DNA,
part of why she is so good is she very

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.640 position:58%
much wants to instill that continuous
improvement.

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:12.770 position:56%
It's about operational efficiency.
It's about doing things smarter with

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:15.260 position:56%
less.
And I always talk about it as it's a

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:19.760 position:61%
lifestyle, not a diet.
So let's not go on programs and look to

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:23.840
cut this much and cut that much.
It's about continuous improvement,

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.900 position:56%
operating efficiently and having the
right approach.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.680 position:56%
And if you do that and you have that
lifestyle, then you're actually

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:34.130 position:58%
generating funds so you can fund that
growth.

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:38.870 position:63%
And so it's a flywheel and it's the
approach that we've been taking and it's

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:43.400 position:64%
allowed us to grow our businesses faster.
To drive productivity and improve

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.530
efficiency,
Prudential has made significant

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:48.770
investments in data analytics and
artificial intelligence.

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:53.390
Yanela Frias plays a key role in
integrating these tools into the

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:56.420 position:66%
company's processes.
We always need people. We're in the people

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:59.690
business. But more and more, if we
really want to provide the right

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:05.390
customer experience, fast access to
products, it's about technology.

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:09.530 position:59%
Technology has always been an enabler.
Now it's a strategic differentiator.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:15.200 position:63%
We expect that by 2027, AI spending will
be about half a trillion dollars between

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:18.320
big tech and corporates.
And so you'll see an arms race to

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.550 position:63%
develop all of these excellent
generative AI solutions that can be used

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:26.180 position:59%
opportunistically by life insurance
companies who are usually a little bit

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:29.270
more conservative.
But there are many applications for

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:34.580 position:59%
them, including servicing, claims,
processing and just generally reducing

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.990 position:61%
paperwork and cost.
So we expect that the AI arms race will

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:41.990
be a huge tailwind for the life
insurance company over time.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:46.640 position:56%
Give me a sense of AI in particular.
What are you experimenting with?

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.650
A few areas.
One is around customer experience.

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:55.160 position:59%
In our individual life business.
Our underwriting process has gone from

00:08:55.370 --> 00:09:01.190 position:63%
about 20 days to minutes.
In our group business, we have partnered

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:05.150 position:59%
with a number of different fintechs to
really improve customer experience, to

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:10.340 position:63%
bring on board capabilities, things like
claims management, where we can process

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:14.120
a disability claim faster and help
people get to work faster.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:18.050 position:56%
How much productivity uplift are you
getting and will you get?

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:22.370 position:56%
We're probably getting 10% at this
point, but I think over time that is

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:27.740 position:56%
where the value is and it will then
allow us to take our capacity in the

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:31.640 position:59%
form of people and redirect it to more
value add activities.

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:35.690
In a nutshell, it means turning the
menial into meaningful.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:42.710 position:63%
It can be both a huge saver of time, but
it can also provide real insight.

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:46.790 position:61%
Now where we're going with it.
Let's say you want to see the financial

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:50.840 position:56%
data every month.
GEN AI will put inferences and say I

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:55.550 position:63%
see trends over the past three years and
here's what I'm seeing and why.

00:09:56.030 --> 00:10:01.400 position:64%
And it will provide prompts for for our
managers to think about, different issues

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.650
that they might not see, sort of
connecting the dots.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.630 position:58%
For all the intriguing potential of
A.I., Prudential has got a rock solid

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:11.270
asset right at hand.
It's the balance sheet.

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:14.690
Are you 'fortress Balance sheet
first' as a CFO?

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:18.320
Yes, absolutely.
My number one job is capital and

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:22.520 position:61%
liquidity.
A Double A credit rating and $4 billion

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:26.000
plus in liquidity gives the company
enviable flexibility.

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:31.040 position:56%
We have a really strong foundation.
At the same time, there are gaps and

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:35.300 position:58%
capabilities that we need to either
build ourselves or acquire, and we're

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:37.670
doing some of both in our asset
management business.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:42.020 position:61%
Yeah, we are looking to enhance
capabilities in private credit in a few

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:45.170 position:56%
key areas.
And then internationally in emerging

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:48.050
markets, we would be looking for
opportunities to expand as well.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:50.540 position:64%
So is that why you'd like to have a
little bit of extra on the balance sheet?

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:52.880
It's always
good to have a little extra.

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.420 position:63%
But then that suggests that you're ready
to act quite nimbly if those

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:57.950
opportunities come.
Yes.

00:10:57.950 --> 00:10:59.880
Because you never know when the
opportunities come.

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:03.500
Right.
So having financial flexibility is

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:08.150 position:63%
about being able to weather difficult
environments and markets can change very

00:11:08.150 --> 00:11:11.210 position:59%
quickly.
But it's also about having the capital

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:15.020
and the currency to act when
opportunities come as well.

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:20.150 position:63%
Prudential has reliably returned capital
to shareholders through dividends and

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:25.280 position:63%
share repurchases.
2023 marked the 16th consecutive year of

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:28.700
its increased dividend.
We've kept the level of buybacks

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:33.920 position:63%
constant for a number of years and we've
raised the dividend slowly over time.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:38.210 position:63%
And that's been our philosophy and we've
we've stuck to it.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:43.160 position:63%
Is that something that you have believed
is a counterbalance perhaps to the

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:47.300 position:58%
less aggressive equity performance?
I would have liked, frankly, a better

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:50.060
equity performance than we had.
But we were going through our

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.790 position:63%
transformation.
And as a result, I think investors had a

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:56.150 position:61%
kind of wait and see attitude about are
you going to deliver on the

00:11:56.150 --> 00:11:57.980 position:58%
transformation and when is that going
to,

00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:00.080
when's that going to come into the
numbers?

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:03.950 position:59%
Which again, I understand. I had
conversations with investors about it.

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:07.250
They say what you're doing is
interesting, but I want to see the

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:10.280 position:63%
results of that. And the results of that
are going to come in the next few years.

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:16.250 position:59%
Yanela Frias has spent her entire 27
year professional career at Prudential

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:20.150
and held nearly a dozen jobs at the
company on the way to her current

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:24.260
position as CFO.
She traces her persistence and

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:27.890 position:61%
resilience to her experience emigrating
from Cuba to the U.S.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:32.450 position:63%
with her family at age eight.
When you're that young and you have such

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:37.940 position:59%
a significant change in your life,
you experience your parents risking so

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:42.080 position:58%
much for a better life and to provide
their children with a better life.

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:45.360 position:58%
I think it gave me perspective from a
young age.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:50.030 position:59%
It made me very adaptable to change.
It made me really open to taking risks

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:53.900
because risks led to better
opportunities and to good outcomes.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.410 position:59%
You know, I was the first person in my
family to go to college.

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:59.690 position:56%
And to this day, every time I get a
promotion, every time something good

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:03.290 position:56%
happens at work, my dad always says,
that's why I came to this country.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:06.380
How does that translate to how you
manage as a CFO?

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:09.260 position:63%
I know you've been in different parts of
the business, but do you think that

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:14.420 position:59%
experience, that immigrant experience,
defines how you manage the business?

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:17.090
It does.
I mean, I'm definitely thoughtful,

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:20.730 position:58%
prudent, and I like to understand the
risks, risks I'm taking.

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:25.250 position:58%
But at the end of the day,
you've got this great perspective and

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:28.970 position:69%
you do fear less.
And I always think about it as we either are

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:34.100 position:59%
going to succeed in terms of we made a
decision and it played out well or we

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:35.660
will succeed because I learned
something.

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:43.100 position:56%
Frias is also a path breaker as the
first female CFO in Prudential's 150

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:46.520 position:61%
year history.
Sometimes in my position, I don't think

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:49.370 position:61%
about it too much.
But as I have conversations with women,

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:53.540 position:63%
actually, even when the announcement
went out, the number of emails and texts

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:58.160 position:63%
I got from women throughout this
organization not just congratulating me,

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:02.630 position:59%
but but really acknowledging that this
was a sign that anything was possible

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:04.700 position:58%
and that they too, could rise through
the ranks.

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:08.750 position:61%
I think that was really powerful and
obviously personally for me, having two

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:13.190 position:61%
daughters being the role model for them
and all their friends, it's a great

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:16.670 position:61%
thing.
As Prudential's head of finance, Yanela

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:22.340 position:72%
Frias oversees more than 15 billion dollars in
annual retirement income payments to

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:25.820
more than 2 million individual and
institutional customers.

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:31.460 position:63%
And she empathizes with the struggle for
security in retirement in an era when

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:36.470 position:72%
pension plans have largely given way to 401Ks.
The challenge is

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:40.420
The 401K was always a tax
advantaged savings vehicle.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:42.890
It was never meant to replace the
defined benefit plan.

00:14:42.890 --> 00:14:46.970 position:63%
And so what we need to do is help
individuals understand, yes, you need to

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:51.140 position:61%
save in your 401K plan, but then how do
we turn that into protected income,

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:55.040
Lifetime income?
In this environment, annuities are

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:58.940 position:56%
gaining traction as a solution, with
sales booming across the entire

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:01.670
industry.
The baby boomers are retiring and

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:05.510 position:59%
they're really right now, almost today
at that peak age, people at that

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:08.480
retirement age need protection from
downside in the markets.

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:10.490 position:61%
And so annuities are a great way to get
that.

00:15:11.030 --> 00:15:14.450 position:59%
And longer term, they're going to need
an income replacement.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:18.740 position:59%
We have been in the accumulation mode.
Now we're hitting peak 65 in the U.S.,

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:22.640
but think about Japan as well.
We're in the decumulation mode and

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:26.430 position:59%
people need security of income as they
go forward.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:30.680 position:61%
They need lifetime income.
Are annuities back in vogue and here to

00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:32.660
stay?
They are.

00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:36.230 position:63%
And I think they're here to stay because
they're a great vehicle to create that

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:38.810
protected income.
And one of the good things is that

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:42.590 position:59%
annuities have actually gotten simpler
through the cycle, right?

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:45.500 position:59%
So that we went through the period of
the guaranteed benefits and the living

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:49.730
benefits and annuities became very
complex and expensive, to be fair.

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:53.780 position:58%
The simplification of
the products, less expensive, I think

00:15:53.780 --> 00:15:55.490 position:59%
that's what what's really brought them
back.

00:15:56.300 --> 00:15:59.510 position:61%
Prudential is a major player in another
robust retirement market:

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:04.370 position:59%
Pension risk transfer. Deal volume has
soared in this space where employers

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:08.780 position:56%
sell their in-house pension plans to
insurers, reducing the risk on their

00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:12.020 position:61%
books and helping preserve benefits for
retirees.

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:16.850 position:63%
You have organizations that are not
insurance companies running an insurance

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:20.330 position:63%
business on their balance sheet when you
think about the large organizations and

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:23.690
their pension balances.
And so what a pension risk transfer

00:16:23.690 --> 00:16:28.250 position:56%
transaction does is it transfers the
liability and the obligation to an

00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:33.020 position:63%
insurance company that's better equipped
to really manage that and ensure that

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:35.390
those payments are getting to the
annuitants.

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:41.240
We've done seven of the largest ten
deals in the US, and there's a

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:46.070 position:61%
tremendous opportunity.
There's $3 trillion in pension balances

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:48.530
in the US.
Where do you see the growth in that

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:51.500
pension transfer, that pension risk
transfer?

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:53.360
Are there big opportunities beyond
borders?

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:58.620 position:59%
Yeah, at some point we think there are
pension risk transfer opportunities

00:16:58.650 --> 00:17:01.880 position:56%
beyond borders.
Today we participate in the UK space

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:06.020 position:59%
through longevity reinsurance.
We've also participated in Netherlands

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:09.710 position:59%
again through longevity reinsurance.
We think at some point in Japan that's

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:14.359 position:64%
an opportunity.
And in Brazil we think over time -- today

00:17:14.359 --> 00:17:17.240 position:56%
we're in life insurance.
But over time the products that will

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:20.890 position:61%
evolve as well.
How do you drive this business forward?

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:23.750
You've got a great opportunity.
You're sitting into the CFO seat.

00:17:24.079 --> 00:17:28.369 position:58%
You can shape this in your own image.
What's that going to be?

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:32.270 position:63%
Well, I think a big part of it is how do
we source capital?

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:37.520 position:61%
The reality is there's not enough
capital in insurance companies today to

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:39.680
meet the need.
When you think about pension risk

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:44.210 position:61%
transfer, individual annuities.
And so one of the things we're thinking

00:17:44.210 --> 00:17:46.580
about is how do we source capital
differently?

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:49.240
So that's where third party capital
comes into play.

00:17:49.250 --> 00:17:53.960 position:59%
So we have a sponsored entity, Prismic
in Bermuda, which is our way of

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:57.920 position:63%
accessing third party capital to
continue to bring those solutions to the

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:01.820 position:61%
market.
2025 is a milestone year for Prudential

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:04.940
as the company celebrates its 150th
anniversary.

00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:09.800 position:64%
It's also a year of transition.
Charles Lowrey is set to step down as CEO

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:13.190 position:59%
at the end of March.
He'll step up to the role of executive

00:18:13.190 --> 00:18:16.730 position:63%
chairman.
I said to the board when I came in, Give

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:19.250 position:58%
me five years and we'll transform the
company.

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:23.630 position:63%
And it's been six years.
And I think this is a great time to pass

00:18:23.630 --> 00:18:27.980 position:56%
the baton on to the next leadership.
Prudential's new CEO will be Andrew

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:31.370 position:58%
Sullivan, who's been with the company
for over a decade.

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:35.930 position:61%
He and the CFO go way back.
Andy and I have worked together in many

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:38.170
different ways and shapes over the
years.

00:18:38.180 --> 00:18:41.930 position:59%
That's the wonderful thing about being
in an organization so long.

00:18:42.050 --> 00:18:47.390 position:59%
Actually, fun fact: he and I were both
participants at Prudential's inaugural

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:49.730 position:61%
executive development program ten years
ago.

00:18:49.850 --> 00:18:51.500 position:56%
He's someone I have a lot of respect
for.

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:53.570
He's a great leader.
He's a friend.

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:58.340 position:56%
And I think I look forward to really
partnering with him.

00:18:58.370 --> 00:19:00.920 position:63%
What do you think he's going to bring to
the role of CEO?

00:19:01.250 --> 00:19:06.620 position:61%
I think Andy brings a new perspective,
energy, a thoughtful approach to how we

00:19:06.860 --> 00:19:09.950 position:58%
really look at our opportunities on a
global basis.

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:12.950
And I think there's incredible
opportunities ahead of us.

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:14.830
How are you going to guide this
transition?

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:17.600 position:61%
What kind of executive chairman are you
going to be for 18 months?

00:19:18.530 --> 00:19:23.210 position:61%
I will be a very supportive executive
chairman who will be in the background.

00:19:23.450 --> 00:19:27.200
I have taken a page out of James
Gorman's book, who was executive

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:31.760 position:61%
chairman, and he had a series of rules.
He said, don't ever come talk

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:33.710 position:59%
to me if you haven't talked to the CEO
first.

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:36.290 position:59%
Andy is the CEO of this company and he
will run it

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:40.880 position:63%
2025 marks
Yanela Frias's 28th year with Prudential

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:43.370 position:61%
and the completion of her first year as
CFO.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:46.670 position:63%
I asked her what she sees when she looks
into the future.

00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:52.880 position:63%
What is the biggest opportunity for this
organization, Prudential, in the next

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:55.460
decade?
It's really the global retirement

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:59.720
opportunity.
There's just so much in terms of an

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:06.740 position:61%
aging population, a lack of ability for
the governments to really continue to

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:12.290 position:61%
support retirement and a need for
individuals to have the ability to save

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:16.010
and then generate protected income.
What is the biggest headwind or

00:20:16.010 --> 00:20:18.620 position:58%
challenge for the organisation in the
next ten years?

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:21.440 position:58%
Our biggest challenge is prioritizing
our opportunities.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:26.330 position:63%
There is a lot that we can do, but we
have limited time, limited resources and

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:29.920 position:59%
we really need to focus and that's
something that we work really hard on.

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:34.070 position:63%
How do we prioritize to deliver the best
value to our customers and our

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:36.010
stakeholders?
Complete this sentence.

00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:42.440 position:66%
The hardest part of being a CFO is -- it's
the vast responsibilities, the need

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:47.660 position:61%
to be engaged in different topics.
You know, in any given day, I can be in

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:50.430
five meetings about five different
businesses.

00:20:50.450 --> 00:20:53.660 position:61%
That takes a lot of energy and a lot of
knowledge, but it's what makes the job

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:56.750 position:63%
so much fun and so interesting.
What's the one piece of advice you would

00:20:56.750 --> 00:21:02.030 position:63%
give to every young aspiring CFO?
The piece of advice I would give them is

00:21:02.030 --> 00:21:06.710 position:63%
build relationships in your role.
It is a relationship business at the end

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:10.430 position:63%
of the day.
It's easier to say no to something or to

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:14.330 position:63%
challenge something when you have a
relationship with an individual and that

00:21:14.330 --> 00:21:17.330 position:59%
goes a long way.
And tell me, if you look backwards and

00:21:17.330 --> 00:21:18.950 position:56%
you have to give yourself a piece of
advice

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:21.440 position:59%
when you were younger, what would that
be?

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:24.890 position:59%
I would tell myself not to take myself
so seriously.

00:21:25.550 --> 00:21:30.500 position:61%
I've always been a perfectionist, and
over the years, as I've grown wiser and

00:21:30.500 --> 00:21:34.400
older, I've realized, you know,
perspective is important.

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:37.250
Not everything
has to be perfect.

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:41.510 position:63%
And it's a lot easier to do your job and
do it well when you're more relaxed

00:21:41.510 --> 00:21:43.460 position:59%
about that.
And if a friend called you up and they

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:46.880
said, Listen, great news.
I've just been appointed as CFO --

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:50.060 position:59%
probably after you say
congratulations, you probably stop and

00:21:50.060 --> 00:21:53.930 position:63%
take a breath.
And then you would say? I would say it's

00:21:53.930 --> 00:21:57.800 position:63%
a wonderful opportunity to really impact
the organization.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:01.850 position:58%
You know, I always say to people, I
consider myself the chief instigating

00:22:01.850 --> 00:22:05.870 position:56%
officer because my job is to be
provocative, to challenge the status

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:10.400 position:58%
quo, to force us to think differently
about our strategy and about our

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:12.890 position:59%
businesses.
And that's what's so interesting about

00:22:12.890 --> 00:22:14.660
this role.
I'm Manus Cranny.

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:16.190 line:92%
This is Bloomberg.

